    text: "## Architecture of the Indian Judiciary\n\nEvery piece of evidence collected by a nurse and every injury charted in the MLC register is ultimately destined for the courtroom. To understand where a medico-legal trial takes place, you must understand the vertical pyramid of the Indian Criminal Judiciary.\n\nAt the foundational grassroots level sit the **Magistrates' Courts**. A **Judicial Magistrate Second Class (JMSC)** can pass a sentence of imprisonment up to **1 year** and a small fine. Above them sits the [[Judicial Magistrate First Class (JMFC)]] (called a Metropolitan Magistrate in metropolitan cities), who can award imprisonment up to **3 years** and a fine up to ₹10,000 (increased to ₹50,000 under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita / BNSS). Above the JMFC sits the **Chief Judicial Magistrate (CJM)**, who can pass sentences of imprisonment up to **7 years**.\n\nAbove the Magistrates sits the district's apex trial forum: the [[Sessions Court]], presided over by a District and Sessions Judge. Serious medico-legal crimes—such as Murder, Rape, Dowry Death, and Acid Attacks—are committed directly to the Sessions Court. A Sessions Judge can impose **any sentence authorized by law, including Life Imprisonment and the Death Penalty** (Capital Punishment), with the single constitutional check that any Death Sentence passed by a Sessions Judge must be confirmed by the **High Court** before execution. At the pinnacle of the pyramid sits the **Supreme Court of India** in New Delhi, whose rulings bind every court and hospital across the nation under Article 141.\n\n[[[Clinical Pearl: When a nurse or doctor receives a court **Summons (Subpoena)** to testify in a criminal or civil case, attending court is mandatory. If a clinician is scheduled for hospital duty, a criminal court summons always takes legal precedence over a civil court summons, and a Higher Court takes precedence over a Lower Court. Ignoring a criminal subpoena without a valid medical emergency can result in a Bailable or Non-Bailable Warrant for the clinician's arrest.]]]\n\n[PAGE]\n\n## Substantive & Procedural Criminal Law: IPC/BNS & CrPC/BNSS\n\nIndian criminal law is divided into two engines: **Substantive Law** (the **Indian Penal Code, 1860 [IPC]**, transitioned to the **Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 [BNS]**), which defines what acts are crimes and what punishments they carry; and **Procedural Law** (the **Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 [CrPC]**, transitioned to the **Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 [BNSS]**), which governs how police investigate, how doctors examine, and how courts try those crimes.\n\nIn clinical practice, accurately classifying a patient's injury as 'Simple' or 'Grievous' directly dictates the criminal charge filed against the attacker. Under **Section 320 IPC (Section 116 BNS)**, an injury is legally classified as [[Grievous Hurt]] ONLY if it falls into one of **eight strict anatomical categories**: 1) Emasculation (depriving a male of masculine vigor), 2) Permanent privation of the sight of either eye, 3) Permanent privation of the hearing of either ear, 4) Privation of any member or joint, 5) Destruction or permanent impairing of the powers of any member or joint, 6) Permanent disfiguration of the head or face (such as a deep facial scar or acid burn), 7) Fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth, or 8) Any hurt which **endangers life** or causes the sufferer to be during the space of **20 days** (reduced to **15 days** under BNS) in severe bodily pain or unable to follow their ordinary daily pursuits.\n\n[[[Clinical Pearl: Essential IPC (and BNS) Sections every forensic nurse must know cold for exams and clinical practice: **Section 300/302 IPC (101/103 BNS)** = Murder; **Section 304A IPC (106 BNS)** = Causing death by rash or negligent act (the exact section used to prosecute doctors and nurses for fatal medical negligence, carrying up to 2 years imprisonment); **Section 304B IPC (80 BNS)** = Dowry Death (unnatural death of a woman within 7 years of marriage with cruelty over dowry); **Section 312–316 IPC (88–92 BNS)** = Illegal miscarriage and feticide; **Section 375/376 IPC (63/64 BNS)** = Rape; and **Section 166B IPC (200 BNS)** = Up to 1 year imprisonment for any hospital personnel who refuses free emergency treatment to a sexual assault or acid attack survivor.]]]\n\n{{Under procedural law (CrPC / BNSS), when a body is found dead under suspicious circumstances, an **Inquest** (preliminary inquiry into the apparent cause of death) is triggered. A standard **Police Inquest (Section 174 CrPC / 194 BNSS)** is conducted by an officer not below the rank of Sub-Inspector in the presence of two respectable neighborhood witnesses (*Panchas*). However, in cases of **Custodial Death, Police Firing, or Dowry Death within 7 years of marriage**, a police officer is forbidden from holding the inquest alone; it must be a **Magistrate's Inquest (Section 176 CrPC / 196 BNSS)**, which carries vastly superior impartiality.}}\n\n[PAGE]\n\n## The Indian Evidence Act (IEA / BSA) & Dying Declaration\n\nInside the courtroom, the **Indian Evidence Act, 1872 (IEA)** (transitioned to the **Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 [BSA]**) governs what facts can be legally proved. Witnesses are divided into two classes: a **Common Witness (Witness of Fact)**, who can only testify to what they personally saw or heard with their own senses, and an [[Expert Witness]] under **Section 45 IEA (Section 39 BSA)**—a specialist in medicine, forensic science, handwriting, or finger impressions who is legally permitted to offer **professional opinions and deductions** (such as whether a stab wound could have been self-inflicted or whether hypoxia caused brain death).\n\nNormally, 'hearsay evidence' (repeating what someone else said outside the courtroom) is strictly inadmissible because the speaker is not under oath and cannot be cross-examined. However, Indian law makes a monumental life-and-death exception under **Section 32(1) IEA (Section 26(a) BSA)**: the [[Dying Declaration]]. Based on the legal maxim *Nemo moriturus praesumitur mentiri* ('A person will not meet their Maker with a lie in their mouth'), any verbal, written, or gestural statement made by a person explaining the cause of their death or the circumstances that led to their death becomes powerful legal evidence if the person subsequently dies.\n\n{{The protocol for recording a Dying Declaration in the ICU or burn ward is unforgiving. If time permits, the hospital must immediately summon an **Executive or Judicial Magistrate** to record the statement. However, if the patient is rapidly deteriorating into hemorrhagic or septic shock and cannot survive until a Magistrate arrives, **the attending Doctor or Nurse must record the Dying Declaration immediately** in the presence of two witnesses.}}\n\n[[[Clinical Pearl: Before a single word of a Dying Declaration is recorded—even by a Magistrate—the attending doctor MUST examine the patient and write a formal certificate on the paper stating that the patient is **Compos Mentis** (conscious, oriented, and mentally fit to give a valid statement) both before and throughout the recording. Record the patient's exact vernacular words in Question-and-Answer format without leading questions, read it back to them, and take their signature or left thumb impression. If the patient miraculously survives their injuries, the Dying Declaration loses its status under Section 32(1) and can only be used as a corroborative statement under Section 157 IEA!]]]\n\n[PAGE]\n\n## Protecting the Child: The POCSO Act, 2012\n\nFor decades, the Indian Penal Code failed children: traditional rape laws only recognized female victims, ignored non-penetrative sexual abuse, and forced terrified children into hostile adult courtrooms. To close this gap, Parliament enacted the [[POCSO Act]] (**Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012**), amended in 2019 to introduce even harsher penalties including the Death Penalty for aggravated penetrative assault on children.\n\nThe POCSO Act is completely **gender-neutral**—protecting both boys and girls—and defines a 'Child' as **any person below the age of 18 years**. Because the legal age of consent in India is strictly 18 years, any sexual act involving a person under 18 is statutorily classified as sexual assault under POCSO, even if the adolescent claims it was 'consensual.' The Act categorizes crimes by severity: Penetrative Sexual Assault (Section 3), Aggravated Penetrative Sexual Assault (Section 5—committed by police, doctors/nurses, teachers, family members, or on a child below 12 years), Sexual Assault (non-penetrative touching with sexual intent, Section 7), and Sexual Harassment / Using a child for pornography.\n\n{{Section 19 and Section 21 of the POCSO Act enforce a strict **Mandatory Reporting Rule** upon every nurse, doctor, hospital administrator, and citizen. If a nurse examines a minor who presents with an STI, pregnancy, or physical signs of sexual abuse—and fails to report it to the Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU) or local police—the nurse commits a punishable criminal offense carrying up to **6 months imprisonment and/or a fine**, even if the child's parents beg the nurse to keep it secret to protect family honor.}}\n\n[[[Clinical Pearl: Under **Section 27 of the POCSO Act**, the medical examination of a child survivor must follow strict child-friendly rules: 1) It must be conducted even BEFORE an FIR is registered, 2) In the case of a girl child, the examination MUST be conducted exclusively by a **female doctor**, 3) It must be performed in the physical presence of the parent or a trusted person whom the child has confidence in, and 4) If no parent or trusted relative is present, the head of the hospital must designate a trained **female health worker or support person** to remain with the child throughout the entire examination.]]]"
